{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.GroundRing","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.GroundRing","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Laurent","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Int.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.ZMod.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.QIntegerCanonical"],"declarations":13,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.ofPairs","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.ofPairs","kind":"def","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GroundRing.lean#L124-L134","doc":"Build a Laurent polynomial from a list of `(exponent, coefficient)`\npairs: `Σᵢ cᵢ · T^{kᵢ}`.  Folding over a flat `List (ℤ × ℤ)` lets Lean\nelaborate the `•`/`+` operations **once** (in the fold body) instead of\nbuilding a deep `HAdd`/`HSMul` expression tree per definition — the latter\ndoes not finish elaborating within practical time/heartbeat budgets for\nthese hecke-engine witness polynomials.\n\nShared helper for the `QOU.Archimedean.PauliWitness` modules; centralised\nhere from the previously per-file `private` copies. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.two_E_cross_eq_HA","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.two_E_cross_eq_HA","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GroundRing.lean#L121-L122","doc":"`2 · E_cross = q − q⁻¹ = HA`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.two_E_cross","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.two_E_cross","kind":"def","line":114,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GroundRing.lean#L114-L119","doc":"The crossing energy `E_cross = ½(q − q⁻¹)`.\n\n**Note**: the factor ½ takes us outside ℤ[q, q⁻¹] into\nℤ[½][q, q⁻¹].  For formal proofs we work with `2 · E_cross = HA`\nto stay in the ground ring.  The ½ is a presentation convenience. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.hbarQ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.hbarQ","kind":"def","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GroundRing.lean#L110-L112","doc":"The quantum Planck constant `ℏ_q = (q − 1)/q = 1 − q⁻¹`.\nThis is the action quantum per exceptional divisor step. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HA","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HA","kind":"def","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GroundRing.lean#L106-L108","doc":"The Hecke amplitude `HA = q − q⁻¹`.  This is the coefficient\nin the Hecke relation `σ² = HA · σ + 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.qInt_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.qInt_two","kind":"theorem","line":99,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GroundRing.lean#L99-L102","doc":"`[2]_q = q + q⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.qInt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.qInt_one","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GroundRing.lean#L94-L97","doc":"`[1]_q = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.qInt_eq_geo_twist","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.qInt_eq_geo_twist","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GroundRing.lean#L64-L92","doc":"**Bridge to the canonical bracket — the twist, made division-free.**\n\nThis is the one q-integer in the corpus that genuinely *cannot* be an instance\nof `qIntBal`: `R_q = ℤ[q, q⁻¹]` is not a field, so the balanced quotient\n`(qⁿ − q⁻ⁿ)/(q − q⁻¹)` is not even expressible here.  That is why the definition\nabove spells out the symmetric weight string `qⁿ⁻¹ + qⁿ⁻³ + ⋯ + q⁻⁽ⁿ⁻¹⁾` as an\nexplicit sum of monomials instead.\n\nWhat it is *not* is a different convention.  `qIntBal_eq_twist_canonical` says\n`[n]_q = q⁻⁽ⁿ⁻¹⁾ · qIntGeo(q²) n` over a field; `qIntGeo` needs only a\n`CommRing`, so the right-hand side survives the drop to `ℤ[q, q⁻¹]` even though\nthe left-hand side does not.  This theorem is that surviving half — the same\ntwist, stated where no division is available.\n\nProved for `0 < n`; the negative branch is `-` of this by construction\n(`qInt` is defined by odd extension), matching `qIntBalZ_neg` over a field. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.qInt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.qInt","kind":"def","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GroundRing.lean#L50-L62","doc":"The quantum integer `[n]_q = (qⁿ − q⁻ⁿ) / (q − q⁻¹)` as an\nelement of `ℤ[q, q⁻¹]`.  For `n ≥ 0`, this is a polynomial\nin `q` and `q⁻¹` (the denominator divides the numerator). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.q_mul_q_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.q_mul_q_inv","kind":"theorem","line":41,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GroundRing.lean#L41-L46","doc":"`q · q⁻¹ = 1` in `ℤ[q, q⁻¹]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.q_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.q_inv","kind":"def","line":38,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GroundRing.lean#L38-L39","doc":"The inverse `q⁻¹` in `ℤ[q, q⁻¹]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.q","kind":"def","line":35,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GroundRing.lean#L35-L36","doc":"The indeterminate `q` in `ℤ[q, q⁻¹]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.R_q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.R_q","kind":"def","line":28,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GroundRing.lean#L28-L33","doc":"The ground ring for QOU formal proofs.\n`R_q := ℤ[q, q⁻¹]` (Laurent polynomials over ℤ).\n\nIn Lean 4 / Mathlib, this is `LaurentPolynomial ℤ`.\nWe use `q` as the indeterminate. ","signatureChunk":0}]}